Z||000000||fierospeeder||04-17-2001||09:46 PM||||There was a magazine made by "hot rod magazine" called  Tools & Dream Shops.<P><BR>It was tips on building your garage or workshop and the tools you will need, budget and organization.  "one time special issue"<P>In the end of the magazine, it describes, how to install an air line in your garage.<P>1/2 inch PCV pipe was used, that was labled SCH40,  rating of 600psi.  <P>The layout is this, a sears oilless compressor is hooked into a two foot long rubber line which is connected to a pcv pipe(with a valve to drain water).  The line goes up, and across the garage.  There is a steel pipe (steel used for protection because its location near the garage door, and to firmly mount the regulator: noted by author) which goes down the wall into a steel elbow.  The elbow goes into a sears regulator/filter and into your ordinary 25 foot rubber air hose.<P>I saw this article and drooled.  Bought myself 8 10 foot long 40 pcv pipe just like the article.<P>I sometimes go into the home improvement forum and some people mentioned on how they wanted to use pvc for an air line.  Forum members replied in stating things that osha says pcv isn't used for this and pcv will shatter and explode causing a mess and a dangerous situation.  Copper or steel should be used instead of pcv.<P>Other memebers stated that they have used it in their jobs at work, and in their homes, and never had it explode for years.  One guy stated, he worked with it for 4 years and no probs.<P>Im confused by this, would a magazine know better?  They did say this "Note that it is labeled SCH40; this stands for schedule 40, which equates to the thickness of the pipe's wall.  PVC pipe also comes in schedule 80 and schedule 200; these are much thinner walled piping, and should not be used for compressed air systems."<P>I will be using a non oiler system, but will have those little oilers that attach to the air tools, because i will be painting in my garage and sandblasting.   I thought about three outlets, one by the garage door, one by the sand blaster and one on the ceiling for retractable hose. <P>isn't water pressure same as air pressure,  isn't like saying which weighs more 30 lbs of feathers or 30lbs of rocks?  Plus doesn't water have higher demands in piping such has very hot temps to cold temps, PVC does have a rating of 600 psi at 73 degrees?  And i will be running max, 100psi.||||reg||1
Z||000001||85Josh||04-17-2001||11:03 PM||||fierospeeder, the problem isn't with the pipe's ability to withstand pressure, it's because plastic cant handle the vibration of an airline. If you think about it, this stuff is made to be put in a wall and have water run through it, which at worst hammers a few times when you shut it off. Air, on the other hand, is pumped by a reciprocating piston, and is often used with something like an airhammer or nailer that starts and stops more often and much harder than water. Over time, this vibration, combined with the oil in your air, causes the PVC to craze, weaken and crack. Then one day, you shut off your blowgun, and a six-foot section of PVC flies by your ear! I have had this happen in a woodshop where the boss didn't want to spend the money for black iron, and he has the workmans comp bills to show it. <P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierospeeder||04-18-2001||12:27 AM||||is galvanized steel pipe okay?  it costs about 1.5 more then black steel.<P>also would a pipe threader would be needed, never done this before?||||reg||1
Z||000003||rogergarrison||04-18-2001||10:32 AM||||most pipe for airlines is already threaded as you buy it. in my shop i use rated hose everywhere. from my compressor to a Devilbis regulator/water trap. Has multiple outlets at trap and air hoses attached. I like using the hose because ive found that using metal pipe which is cold causes more condensation in air flow than using all hose.<BR> As for your other question, water and air are different in that air can be compressed--water cannot.||||reg||1
Z||000004||c m west||04-18-2001||11:17 AM||||there is absolutley nothing wrong with using PVC||||reg||1
Z||000005||85Josh||04-18-2001||08:43 PM||||Actually, black iron is the best, galvanized can have the galvanizing flake off and tear up air tools.<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||1
Z||000006||FieroJoe||04-18-2001||11:04 PM||||Black iron pipe is code (in my area) and the norm for natural gas.  Galvanize pipe is more expensive and is rated for water.  We have 1/2" black running from our compressor to our regulator and water trap, and from that a tee with a quick connect for 3/8" hose for all of our tools, and then one for 1/2" hose for our HVLP gun.<P>We also have a quick connect at the bottom of the big compressor (60 gal) so we can connect  in our smaller 15 gal compressor if we need more volume.  It works out great, having air, in my opinion, is a definate shop essential.<P>Also, about threading pipe, you may find that you'll need a 10 foot section or whatever that you bought, cut down and re threaded.  At Ace, where I work, we do this for free if you've already bought the pipe.  We charge like $.5 to cut and thread if you bring in your own pipe.  So basically, you probably wont have to go through the trouble of renting a threader and trying to do it by hand if you want to make a few trips to the hardware.<P>Also, I recommend putting in a shut off valve between your line and the compressor, if your compressor already doesnt have one.  This will prevent your entire tank to leak down when not in use in case you have a small leak at one of your connections.   Get a ball valve, not a gated valve.<P>For your threaded connections you can use either thread tape or dope, but I recommend the dope to all my customers working with gas or air.  Tape seems to work fine with water.  Pipe dope has a high pressure rating and is thicker, so it fills the threads better.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||alpha||04-19-2001||09:18 AM||||I just setup my shop air using 1/2" type M copper using sweat fittings. <P>I read about someone who had done it in their shop and highly recommended it, but I was not sure if the soldered together fittings could take it. I have since visited a number of professional auto shops which used copper. If it is good enough for them it is good enough for me.<P>It is really cheap as well at about $3 for 10' of 1/2" Type M. It is a lot less work then cutting and threading iron pipe if you ask me.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://fiero.dhs.org/fiero/sig.jpg">   <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu">douglas_knight@umit.maine.edu</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||alpha||04-19-2001||09:21 AM||||Fiero Joe - you work at an Ace Hardware? ....I have worked at one part time for about 4 years.||||reg||1
